---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptPage from "../../../components/PromptPage.astro";

const masterPrompt = `Act as an expert email marketing strategist, lifecycle marketer, conversion copywriter and campaign planner.

TASK:
Create a complete email marketing campaign for the supplied product, service, offer, content asset or business objective.

The campaign should:
- match the audience and customer stage
- use a clear campaign objective
- sequence emails logically
- provide value before pushing for action where appropriate
- use truthful subject lines and claims
- avoid spammy language
- maintain consistent brand voice
- include practical testing and measurement guidance

BUSINESS / BRAND:
[Name and short description.]

PRODUCT / SERVICE / OFFER:
[What is being promoted?]

TARGET AUDIENCE:
[Who will receive the campaign?]

AUDIENCE STAGE:
[New subscriber / lead / prospect / customer / inactive customer / trial user / other.]

CAMPAIGN OBJECTIVE:
[Welcome / nurture / educate / launch / generate leads / sell / reactivate / onboard / retain / other.]

PRIMARY CUSTOMER PROBLEM:
[What problem matters most?]

DESIRED OUTCOME:
[What does the customer want?]

VALUE PROPOSITION:
[Why should they care?]

KEY BENEFITS:
[List supported benefits.]

KEY FEATURES:
[List relevant real features.]

OFFER:
[Discount / free trial / download / consultation / bundle / no offer / other.]

PROOF AVAILABLE:
[Testimonials / reviews / data / case study / demonstration / credentials / none.]

PRIMARY CTA:
[Buy / book / download / visit / try / reply / sign up / other.]

EMAIL LIST SOURCE:
[Newsletter signup / lead magnet / customer list / event / product signup / other.]

CAMPAIGN LENGTH:
[Number of emails or let AI recommend.]

CAMPAIGN PERIOD:
[For example: 5 days / 2 weeks / 30 days.]

BRAND TONE:
[Professional / practical / friendly / educational / premium / conversational / other.]

PERSONALIZATION AVAILABLE:
[Name / company / product interest / purchase history / none.]

RESTRICTIONS:
[Legal / compliance / brand / product / timing restrictions.]

SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS:
[Any additional instructions.]

EMAIL CAMPAIGN REQUIREMENTS:

1. START WITH THE CAMPAIGN GOAL

2. DEFINE THE AUDIENCE STAGE

3. DO NOT SEND THE SAME MESSAGE TO EVERY TYPE OF SUBSCRIBER

4. IDENTIFY THE PRIMARY CUSTOMER MOTIVATION

5. IDENTIFY THE MAIN OBJECTION

6. IDENTIFY THE NEXT LOGICAL ACTION

7. BUILD A SEQUENCE RATHER THAN A COLLECTION OF RANDOM EMAILS

8. EACH EMAIL SHOULD HAVE ONE PRIMARY PURPOSE

9. POSSIBLE EMAIL PURPOSES:
Welcome
Education
Problem recognition
Value explanation
Demonstration
Use case
Trust
Objection handling
Offer
Reminder
Follow-up
Reactivation

10. CREATE A LOGICAL FLOW

11. DEFAULT FLOW WHEN APPROPRIATE:
Email 1 — Context / value
Email 2 — Problem / education
Email 3 — Solution / use case
Email 4 — Proof / objection
Email 5 — CTA / offer

12. DO NOT FORCE FIVE EMAILS IF FEWER ARE BETTER

13. DO NOT CREATE AN OVERLONG SEQUENCE FOR A SIMPLE OFFER

14. MATCH SEQUENCE LENGTH TO:
Offer complexity
Customer risk
Purchase cycle
Audience familiarity
Campaign goal

15. CREATE A STRONG SUBJECT LINE FOR EACH EMAIL

16. SUBJECT LINES SHOULD BE:
Clear
Relevant
Specific
Truthful

17. DO NOT USE DECEPTIVE SUBJECT LINES

18. DO NOT IMPLY A PERSONAL REPLY WHEN IT IS A MASS EMAIL

19. DO NOT USE FALSE RE:
or
FWD:
unless it is genuinely part of an existing conversation.

20. DO NOT USE FALSE URGENCY

21. DO NOT USE FALSE SCARCITY

22. DO NOT INVENT DEADLINES

23. DO NOT INVENT DISCOUNTS

24. DO NOT INVENT AVAILABILITY LIMITS

25. DO NOT INVENT CUSTOMER RESULTS

26. DO NOT INVENT TESTIMONIALS

27. DO NOT INVENT RATINGS

28. DO NOT INVENT AWARDS

29. DO NOT INVENT PERFORMANCE CLAIMS

30. DO NOT CLAIM GUARANTEED RESULTS

31. CREATE PREVIEW TEXT

32. PREVIEW TEXT SHOULD ADD TO THE SUBJECT LINE
Do not simply repeat it.

33. CREATE A CLEAR OPENING

34. OPENING SHOULD QUICKLY ESTABLISH RELEVANCE

35. AVOID LONG BRAND INTRODUCTIONS

36. USE CUSTOMER-CENTRIC LANGUAGE

37. DO NOT START EVERY EMAIL WITH:
"We are excited to announce..."

38. CREATE A CLEAR MESSAGE HIERARCHY

39. EMAIL STRUCTURE MAY INCLUDE:
Opening
Problem / context
Value
Supporting explanation
Proof
CTA

40. DO NOT INCLUDE EVERY SECTION IN EVERY EMAIL

41. KEEP ONE PRIMARY CTA

42. SECONDARY LINKS MAY BE USED CAREFULLY

43. DO NOT CREATE MULTIPLE COMPETING ACTIONS

44. MATCH CTA TO CUSTOMER STAGE

45. EARLY-STAGE CTA EXAMPLES:
Read the guide
See how it works
Try the tool
Learn more
Reply

46. HIGH-INTENT CTA EXAMPLES:
Start trial
Buy now
Request quote
Book consultation

47. RETENTION CTA EXAMPLES:
Explore feature
Complete setup
Reorder
Upgrade
Refer

48. CREATE NATURAL EMAIL LENGTH

49. SHORT EMAILS
Use for:
Reminders
Simple offers
Updates
High-awareness audiences

50. LONGER EMAILS
Use for:
Education
Complex products
Story
Objection handling

51. DO NOT ADD LENGTH FOR ITS OWN SAKE

52. CREATE SCANNABLE EMAILS

53. USE:
Short paragraphs
Simple sentences
Useful bullets
Clear CTA

54. AVOID WALLS OF TEXT

55. CREATE WELCOME CAMPAIGNS WHEN REQUESTED

56. WELCOME SEQUENCE SHOULD:
Set expectations
Deliver promised value
Introduce useful resources
Build trust
Guide next step

57. DO NOT IMMEDIATELY HARD-SELL EVERY NEW SUBSCRIBER

58. CREATE NURTURE CAMPAIGNS WHEN REQUESTED

59. NURTURE SHOULD:
Educate
Clarify problem
Demonstrate value
Handle objections
Move toward action

60. CREATE PRODUCT-LAUNCH CAMPAIGNS WHEN REQUESTED

61. LAUNCH EMAILS MAY INCLUDE:
Problem
New solution
Use case
Demo
Offer
Reminder

62. DO NOT CREATE FAKE LAUNCH EXCITEMENT

63. CREATE ONBOARDING CAMPAIGNS WHEN REQUESTED

64. ONBOARDING SHOULD HELP THE USER REACH VALUE QUICKLY

65. POSSIBLE ONBOARDING FLOW:
Welcome
First action
Key feature
Common mistake
Advanced use
Support

66. CREATE REACTIVATION CAMPAIGNS WHEN REQUESTED

67. REACTIVATION SHOULD:
Acknowledge inactivity carefully
Remind of value
Offer useful content
Invite return

68. DO NOT CLAIM TO KNOW WHY THE USER BECAME INACTIVE

69. CREATE ABANDONMENT CAMPAIGNS ONLY WHEN RELEVANT

70. DO NOT INVENT CART OR USER ACTIVITY DATA

71. CREATE SEGMENTATION IDEAS

72. POSSIBLE SEGMENTS:
New subscribers
High-intent leads
Customers
Inactive customers
Product interest
Industry
Role
Purchase stage

73. DO NOT CREATE SENSITIVE OR INAPPROPRIATE SEGMENTATION

74. USE PERSONALIZATION ONLY WHEN DATA EXISTS

75. DO NOT FABRICATE:
First name
Company
Purchase
Location
Behaviour

76. CREATE VALUE BEFORE REPETITION

77. DO NOT SEND THE SAME SALES MESSAGE REPEATEDLY

78. VARY THE REASON TO ENGAGE

79. POSSIBLE CONTENT TYPES:
Tip
Guide
Case example
FAQ
Demo
Checklist
Comparison
Story
Offer

80. CREATE OBJECTION HANDLING

81. COMMON OBJECTIONS:
Price
Trust
Complexity
Time
Fit
Switching
Need for approval

82. ADDRESS OBJECTIONS WITH FACTS

83. DO NOT INVENT GUARANTEES

84. DO NOT INVENT REFUND POLICIES

85. CREATE TRUST ELEMENTS

86. POSSIBLE TRUST:
Real testimonial
Demo
Case study
Methodology
Transparent pricing
Credentials
Support information

87. IF NO PROOF EXISTS
Do not fabricate it.

88. CREATE CTA REINFORCEMENT

89. CTA COPY SHOULD BE SPECIFIC

90. AVOID VAGUE CTA TEXT WHEN A CLEAR ACTION IS AVAILABLE

91. CREATE PLAIN-TEXT-FRIENDLY COPY

92. DO NOT RELY ON HEAVY DESIGN FOR MEANING

93. IMPORTANT INFORMATION SHOULD STILL WORK IN SIMPLE EMAIL FORMAT

94. CREATE MOBILE-FRIENDLY COPY

95. KEEP SUBJECT LINES AND OPENINGS CONCISE

96. KEEP PARAGRAPHS SHORT

97. CREATE A/B TEST IDEAS

98. TEST ONE IMPORTANT VARIABLE AT A TIME

99. POSSIBLE TESTS:
Subject line
Opening
CTA
Offer framing
Email length
Send timing

100. DO NOT TEST EVERYTHING AT ONCE

101. CREATE A TEST HYPOTHESIS

102. DO NOT GUARANTEE RESULTS

103. CREATE PERFORMANCE METRICS

104. MATCH METRIC TO OBJECTIVE

105. POSSIBLE METRICS:
Delivery rate
Open rate
Click-through rate
Reply rate
Conversion rate
Unsubscribe rate
Revenue
Activation rate

106. DO NOT CLAIM UNIVERSAL BENCHMARKS WITHOUT VERIFIED DATA

107. USE THE BUSINESS'S OWN BASELINE WHEN POSSIBLE

108. DO NOT OVERFOCUS ON OPEN RATE
Privacy protections can affect open tracking.

109. PRIORITIZE DOWNSTREAM ACTIONS WHEN POSSIBLE

110. CREATE SEND-TIMING GUIDANCE

111. IF NO DATA EXISTS
Recommend testing rather than claiming a universal best time.

112. DO NOT CLAIM A SPECIFIC BEST DAY OR HOUR WITHOUT evidence.

113. CREATE FREQUENCY GUIDANCE

114. AVOID EXCESSIVE SENDING

115. MATCH FREQUENCY TO:
Campaign urgency
Customer expectation
Content value
Offer lifecycle

116. CREATE UNSUBSCRIBE-AWARE COPY

117. DO NOT USE MANIPULATIVE LANGUAGE TO PREVENT UNSUBSCRIBES

118. CREATE COMPLIANCE CHECKS

119. FLAG:
Misleading subject lines
Missing sender identity
Unsupported claims
Sensitive targeting
Unclear promotional terms
Missing unsubscribe process

120. DO NOT CLAIM LEGAL COMPLIANCE AUTOMATICALLY

121. REMIND USER TO REVIEW APPLICABLE EMAIL AND PRIVACY RULES

122. CREATE SPAM-RISK REVIEW

123. AVOID EXCESSIVE:
ALL CAPS
Exclamation marks
Misleading urgency
Suspicious claims

124. DO NOT TREAT SPECIFIC WORDS AS GUARANTEED SPAM TRIGGERS

125. CREATE LINK STRATEGY

126. USE FEW PURPOSEFUL LINKS

127. PRIMARY CTA SHOULD BE OBVIOUS

128. CREATE DESTINATION ALIGNMENT

129. EMAIL PROMISE SHOULD MATCH THE LANDING PAGE OR RESOURCE

130. DO NOT SEND USERS TO AN UNRELATED PAGE

131. CREATE FOLLOW-UP LOGIC

132. IF USER CLICKS:
Suggest appropriate next-stage follow-up.

133. IF USER DOES NOT CLICK:
Suggest a different value angle rather than repeating the same email.

134. DO NOT ASSUME PERSONAL BEHAVIOUR WITHOUT DATA

135. CREATE CAMPAIGN CALENDAR

136. INCLUDE:
Email number
Purpose
Suggested spacing
CTA
Primary metric

137. USE RELATIVE SPACING WHEN EXACT TIMING IS UNKNOWN

138. EXAMPLE:
Day 0
2 days later
3 days later

139. CREATE CONTENT REUSE OPPORTUNITIES

140. REPURPOSE:
Blog
Guide
Video
FAQ
Case study
Social post

into email content where appropriate.

141. DO NOT DUPLICATE THE FULL SOURCE VERBATIM

142. CREATE FINAL EDITING CHECK

143. REMOVE:
Filler
Repetition
Generic hype
Unnecessary adjectives

144. CHECK BRAND VOICE

145. CHECK CLAIM SUPPORT

146. CHECK CTA CLARITY

147. CHECK SEQUENCE LOGIC

148. CREATE INFORMATION GAPS

149. IDENTIFY FACTS NEEDED BEFORE SEND

150. FINAL QUALITY CHECK
Before finalizing verify:

- campaign objective is clear
- audience stage is clear
- sequence has logical progression
- each email has one primary purpose
- subject lines are truthful
- preview text adds value
- openings establish relevance quickly
- emails are customer-focused
- CTA matches stage
- copy is mobile-readable
- no false urgency is used
- no false scarcity is used
- no offer is invented
- no proof is invented
- no result is invented
- personalization uses only available data
- objections are handled factually
- landing-page message matches the email
- tests change meaningful variables
- metrics match campaign objectives
- unsupported claims are flagged
- email frequency is reasonable
- compliance review is recommended
- final sequence is practical to execute

OUTPUT FORMAT:

1. CAMPAIGN STRATEGY
Provide:
Audience
Audience stage
Objective
Primary problem
Desired outcome
Value proposition
Primary CTA

2. CAMPAIGN SEQUENCE
Create the recommended email sequence.

For each email provide:
Email number
Purpose
Recommended timing
Primary message
CTA

3. SUBJECT LINE OPTIONS
Create 3–5 truthful subject options for each email.

4. PREVIEW TEXT
Provide matching preview text.

5. FULL EMAIL COPY
Write each email with:
Subject
Preview text
Opening
Body
CTA

6. SEGMENTATION OPTIONS
Recommend useful segments when appropriate.

7. PERSONALIZATION OPTIONS
List safe personalization fields based only on available data.

8. OBJECTION EMAIL
Create one email focused on the most important objection if needed.

9. REACTIVATION / FOLLOW-UP LOGIC
Explain how to follow up based on campaign response where relevant.

10. A/B TEST PLAN
Provide:
Element
Control
Variant
Hypothesis
Metric

11. CAMPAIGN CALENDAR
Provide:
Email
Timing
Purpose
CTA
Primary metric

12. METRICS PLAN
Provide:
Primary metric
Secondary metrics
What each metric indicates

13. CLAIM CHECK
List:
Claim
Support status
Risk
Recommended wording

14. DELIVERABILITY & COMPLIANCE CHECK
Identify issues requiring human review.

15. INFORMATION REQUIRED
List any missing information needed before sending.

16. FINAL RECOMMENDATION
Summarize the strongest campaign structure and first action.

IMPORTANT:
- Do not invent offers, discounts, deadlines or scarcity.
- Do not fabricate testimonials, ratings or results.
- Do not use deceptive subject lines.
- Keep each email focused.
- Match the sequence to the audience stage.
- Use personalization only when the data actually exists.
- Review applicable email marketing and privacy requirements before sending.`;

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Give the sequence one objective",
    description:
      "Define whether the campaign is meant to welcome, nurture, educate, launch, sell, onboard or reactivate before deciding what each email should do.",
  },
  {
    title: "Give each email a role",
    description:
      "Build a progression where every message contributes something new instead of sending repeated versions of the same promotional email.",
  },
  {
    title: "Match the CTA to customer readiness",
    description:
      "Early-stage subscribers may need education or a low-friction next step, while higher-intent users can receive a stronger conversion CTA.",
  },
  {
    title: "Test without manufacturing urgency",
    description:
      "Improve subject lines, hooks, CTA wording and sequence timing through controlled tests rather than relying on exaggerated deadlines or scarcity.",
  },
];

const example = {
  input: [
    "Business: Practical inventory tools website.",
    "Audience: Small-business owners and warehouse professionals who downloaded an inventory checklist.",
    "Objective: Nurture subscribers toward using a free Reorder Point Calculator.",
    "Customer problem: Unclear replenishment timing.",
    "Value: Calculator uses demand, lead time and safety stock.",
    "CTA: Use the calculator.",
  ],

  output: [
    "EMAIL 1 — Deliver value: Thank the subscriber for downloading the checklist and give one practical inventory-control tip. CTA: View another useful resource.",
    "EMAIL 2 — Problem education: Explain why reorder timing becomes difficult when demand, supplier lead time and safety stock are considered separately. CTA: See how reorder point works.",
    "EMAIL 3 — Practical solution: Introduce the free calculator and show the three inputs required. CTA: Calculate Your Reorder Point.",
    "EMAIL 4 — Objection support: Explain that the calculator is a planning aid and that real replenishment decisions still depend on data quality and operating conditions. CTA: Try the calculator with one SKU.",
    "SUBJECT TEST: Compare a problem-led subject such as 'When should you reorder this item?' against a task-led subject such as 'Calculate a reorder point from 3 inputs.'",
    "PRIMARY METRIC: Qualified calculator visits or completed calculator use rather than opens alone.",
  ],
};

const tips = [
  {
    title: "A sequence should move the conversation forward",
    description:
      "Each email should add new value, context, proof or decision support rather than repeating the same sales argument several times.",
  },
  {
    title: "Subject lines should earn attention honestly",
    description:
      "Specific relevance and useful curiosity are stronger long-term choices than misleading replies, fake urgency or exaggerated promises.",
  },
  {
    title: "Measure the action that matters",
    description:
      "Clicks, replies, product activation or conversions usually tell you more about business value than open rate by itself.",
  },
];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Email Marketing Campaign Prompt | KRIYANO"
  description="Build structured email marketing campaigns with sequences, subject lines, full email copy, CTAs, segmentation, testing and measurement."
>
  <PromptPage
    category="MARKETING"
    title="Email Marketing Campaign"
    accentTitle="Prompt."
    description="Plan and write complete email sequences for nurture, launch, onboarding, reactivation, education and conversion without relying on spammy or unsupported claims."
    meta={[
      "Free prompt",
      "Email marketing",
      "Campaign sequences",
    ]}
    bestFor={[
      "Lead nurturing",
      "Product launches",
      "Welcome sequences",
      "Customer onboarding",
      "Reactivation",
      "Small businesses",
    ]}
    masterPrompt={masterPrompt}
    introTitle="Build an email sequence that moves the customer forward."
    introDescription="Define the audience stage and campaign objective, then give each email a clear role with truthful subject lines, useful content, focused CTAs and measurable next steps."
    steps={steps}
    exampleTitle="Turn a checklist download into a practical nurture sequence."
    example={example}
    tipsTitle="Good email campaigns earn the next click instead of forcing it."
    tips={tips}
    sidebarTitle="Need a landing page for the campaign?"
    sidebarDescription="Use KRIYANO's Landing Page Messaging Prompt to make sure the destination page continues the same promise, audience message and CTA used in the email."
    warningTitle="Email marketing needs factual and compliance review"
    warningText="Verify offers, deadlines, claims, personalization data and promotional terms before sending. Review applicable email-marketing, privacy and unsubscribe requirements for the locations where you operate."
  />
</BaseLayout>